        <object label="&lt;p dir=&quot;ltr&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Compute cycles: a diagonal wave&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p dir=&quot;ltr&quot;&gt;Each MAC fires exactly once per input vector, on cycle r + c − 1 (r = row, c = column):&lt;/p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;div role=&quot;group&quot; aria-label=&quot;Code&quot; tabindex=&quot;0&quot; dir=&quot;ltr&quot;&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;pre style=&quot;background: transparent; font-family: var(--font-mono);&quot;&gt;&lt;code style=&quot;background: transparent; font-family: var(--font-mono); white-space: pre-wrap; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(230, 230, 230));&quot;&gt;           col1  col2  col3  col4&lt;br&gt;row 1     1     2     3     4&lt;br&gt;row 2     2     3     4     5&lt;br&gt;row 3     3     4     5     6&lt;br&gt;row 4     4     5     6     7&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p dir=&quot;ltr&quot;&gt;This is because x_c enters column c at cycle c and moves down one row per cycle. The partial sum moves right one column per cycle. The two meet at MAC[r][c] on cycle r + c − 1. If x1 to x4 all entered at cycle 1, the x values would reach each MAC out of step with its partial sum, so the sums would be wrong. The staggered entry is called the skew.&lt;/p&gt;" tooltip="Compute wavefront is unchanged. New proposal changes setup bandwidth and routing, not r+c-1 timing. All sixteen weights load simultaneously from one 128-bit line before compute. SRAM is idle during C1..C7. Each cell uses old neighbour registers; x and psum never traverse multiple cells combinationally in one clock." id="p1x-ctrl4">
          <mxCell parent="1" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=10;fillColor=#F1F5FF;strokeColor=#CBD5E1;fontSize=12;" vertex="1">
            <mxGeometry height="285" width="540" x="-100" y="2185" as="geometry" />
          </mxCell>
        </object>
        <mxCell id="p1x-title" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;rounded=0;fontSize=18;" value="&lt;b&gt;Inside the 4 x 4 MAC array&lt;/b&gt; (expands the MAC array block above)" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="40" width="820" x="145" y="2000" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <object label="&lt;b&gt;CPU comparison — measure the full workload&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;A 4x4 layer has 16 useful MAC updates. CPU instruction count is not directly comparable to array clock count.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;CPU latency depends on operand locality, vector/dot-product instructions, issue width, dependencies and clock frequency. Accelerator latency depends on loading, reuse, fill/drain and post-processing.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The 14-edge tile schedule above is a design assumption, not a CPU speedup benchmark. Compare elapsed time and steady-state throughput on the same inputs, arithmetic and memory boundary." tooltip="Earlier approximate CPU cycle counts were removed because no CPU, memory system or instruction sequence was specified. This proposal makes no measured CPU speedup claim." id="IaQo_wawF57eg2LQfPKo-1">
          <mxCell parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;rounded=0;" vertex="1">
            <mxGeometry height="230" width="560" x="70" y="3260" as="geometry" />
          </mxCell>
        </object>
        <object label="CPU / command front end&lt;br&gt;(optional RISC-V core)" tooltip="Optional programmable command source, not a required replacement for the tile FSM. Software or custom/vector instructions submit base_W, base_B, base_CFG, base_X, base_Y and START through command/configuration registers. Address generation, response routing and capture timing remain hardware. No claim that this drawing implements Coral RTL; Coral uses an LSU/vector register file feeding its matrix engine." id="wide-cpu">
          <mxCell parent="1" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;" vertex="1">
            <mxGeometry height="110" width="200" x="100" y="810" as="geometry" />
          </mxCell>
        </object>
        <object label="CMD / START" tooltip="Command/configuration interface only. Native local signals or a command queue may be used; it is not the 128-bit operand path. START is accepted only when idle." id="wide-command">
          <mxCell edge="1" parent="1" source="wide-cpu" style="rounded=0;html=1;endArrow=classic;defaultLabelBackgroundColor=page;edgeStyle=none;jumpStyle=arc;jumpSize=10;exitX=0.5;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;exitPerimeter=0;entryPerimeter=0;" target="fEt4LbSzs0sntKll7tNw-127">
            <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
              <Array as="points" />
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </object>
        <object label="DONE / BUSY" tooltip="BUSY remains active through the final STORE; DONE tells the CPU results are committed. This is status/control, not SRAM data." id="wide-done">
          <mxCell edge="1" parent="1" source="fEt4LbSzs0sntKll7tNw-127" style="rounded=0;html=1;endArrow=classic;defaultLabelBackgroundColor=page;edgeStyle=none;jumpStyle=arc;jumpSize=10;exitX=0;exitY=0.33;entryX=0;entryY=0.5;exitPerimeter=0;entryPerimeter=0;" target="wide-cpu">
            <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
              <Array as="points">
                <mxPoint x="70" y="1060" />
                <mxPoint x="70" y="860" />
              </Array>
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </object>
        <object label="bias[127:0]" tooltip="Four INT32 bias registers drive the four column-1 psum inputs. Bias is already in accumulator units; no additional quantization occurs during loading." id="wide-bias">
          <mxCell edge="1" parent="1" source="fEt4LbSzs0sntKll7tNw-157" style="rounded=0;html=1;endArrow=classic;defaultLabelBackgroundColor=page;edgeStyle=none;jumpStyle=arc;jumpSize=10;exitX=1;exitY=0.9;entryX=0;entryY=0.89;exitPerimeter=0;entryPerimeter=0;" target="fEt4LbSzs0sntKll7tNw-129">
            <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
              <Array as="points">
                <mxPoint x="1000" y="1256" />
                <mxPoint x="1000" y="1290" />
              </Array>
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </object>
        <object label="ReLU enable" tooltip="CFG flags bit0 controls ReLU/bypass. Configuration is captured during setup and held throughout the tile. The line is dedicated local control, not an AXI transaction." id="wide-relu">
          <mxCell edge="1" parent="1" source="fEt4LbSzs0sntKll7tNw-157" style="rounded=0;html=1;endArrow=classic;defaultLabelBackgroundColor=page;edgeStyle=none;jumpStyle=arc;jumpSize=10;exitX=1;exitY=0.14;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;exitPerimeter=0;entryPerimeter=0;" target="fEt4LbSzs0sntKll7tNw-137">
            <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
              <Array as="points">
                <mxPoint x="970" y="1150" />
                <mxPoint x="970" y="770" />
                <mxPoint x="1600" y="770" />
              </Array>
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </object>
        <object label="M[31:0] / S[5:0]" tooltip="CFG M is positive signed INT32, S is 0..62. Scale block uses an INT64 product, nearest rounding with ties to even, then clamps to INT8. Values are model parameters, not per-input dynamic output normalization." id="wide-quant">
          <mxCell edge="1" parent="1" source="fEt4LbSzs0sntKll7tNw-157" style="rounded=0;html=1;endArrow=classic;defaultLabelBackgroundColor=page;edgeStyle=none;jumpStyle=arc;jumpSize=10;exitX=1;exitY=0.36;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;exitPerimeter=0;entryPerimeter=0;" target="fEt4LbSzs0sntKll7tNw-158">
            <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
              <Array as="points">
                <mxPoint x="1010" y="1180" />
                <mxPoint x="1010" y="855" />
                <mxPoint x="1950" y="855" />
              </Array>
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </object>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
